1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Otto Johan Tandefelt – the man and the myth
2 CONT
2 CONT Finnish soldier and nobleman Otto Johan Tandefelt was born in 1782. After the Finnish War in 1810, he was involved in a political conspiracy in Stockholm and in the great riot of 20 June 1810, during which the Marshal of the Realm
2 CONC  of Sweden, Axel von Fersen, was beaten to death. Perhaps because of his Finnish background, Tandefelt was eventually one of the four main suspects in the murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that three of the suspects were of foreign origin and only one was a native of Stockholm says something about the spirit of the times. Otto Johan Tandefelt could never be proved or convicted as the murderer of the Marshal
2 CONC . Nevertheless, he was first held on remand in Stockholm for a year and then, in order to obtain a confession, for almost three years in a fortress prison in Gothenburg. His unspecified prison sentence was commuted to deportation 
2 CONC after a petition for clemency to the king by some military figure. The reason for this exile was likely to have been the fact that he was part of a political conspiracy. The loss of his surname and title was related to this deport
2 CONC ation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1814, Otto Johan Pettersson - using an old wartime pseudonym - was in Spain. There he later enlisted in the army, where he quickly rose to the rank of officer and within a few years to the rank of captain. In the 
2 CONC summer of 1817, he was in his native Finland and met Maria Kristina Dahlman. They had a son in 1818, who was named Carl Fredrik Dahlman. Around 1825, Otto Johan was on his way back to Finland via Stockholm but was arrested and a f
2 CONC ew months later returned to Cádiz in Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course, the rioting crowds in Stockholm in 1810 included all kinds of people. Among those specially dressed as sailors were the city's minor civil servants, such as clerks and notaries, but also shop assistants. However, apart 
2 CONC from the political instability, the real reason for the riot and the brutal act was the sudden death of the heir to the throne, Prince Carl August, who was loved by the people.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were rumours that Carl August had been poisoned and that von Fersen was the culprit. Tandefelt, apparently still loyal to the ruler of Sweden, may have been the odd plainclothes nobleman who watched the rampaging mob at clos
2 CONC e quarters. Given Sergeant Otto Johan Tandefelt's previous intelligence background, it is possible that he might even have been instructed to do so by a relevant military body.
2 CONT
2 CONT Events then spiralled out of control. The story of the murder has been told many times in Sweden. New, striking and fictitious details have been added over the years. But is this how it happened? What do we really know about the h
2 CONC istory of these events? How can a seemingly historical account take on a life of its own when it is retold repeatedly without going back to the original sources? And what happens when accounts are simplified by omitting details wh
2 CONC ich were apparently added later?
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, the City of Stockholm and its education department have their own online information source: Stockholmskällan. It allows for a modern and open-minded investigation of these events, which go back a couple of centuries. It br
2 CONC ings together excellent archival sources, both photographed from the original documents and transcribed in old-fashioned and contemporary language. They are suitable as teaching material for high school history courses, for exampl
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2024, the history of von Fersen's murder was investigated by comparing old original sources with the later mythical story. The criteria for source criticism were proximity, correlation and purpose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT More than four decades after these events in Stockholm, Berndt von Schinkel writes in his apparently fictional narrative in 1852-1853, with vivid dramatization and seemingly careless exaggeration of details, among other things: "H
2 CONC e was an eyewitness to the 1810 assault on von Fersen, although he was not questioned … Tandefelt, an officer who is also a Knight of the Royal Order of the Sword and has received a medal for bravery, was dressed as a sailor. He p
2 CONC ut an end to the terrifying spectacle by jumping onto the victim's chest, breaking his ribcage. At that same moment, Axel von Fersen drew his last breath."
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the beginning of a historical urban legend that has since been quoted in many books with increasingly colourful and racy fictional details. Perhaps von Schinkel, in his writing at the time, for some reason thought that th
2 CONC e Finnish soldier was the most suitable person to play a particularly brutal role.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autopsy report of 30 July 1810, however, the doctors had concluded that the entire strain of the assault caused the death - not any of the individual injuries they found on the body. To link Otto Johan Tandefelt to the crim
2 CONC e, some of the accounts discussed in court focus on what clothes he was wearing on 20 June 1810. Some of those questioned, perhaps to shift responsibility, point to the sailor Otto Johan Tandefelt as the man who had jumped on von 
2 CONC Fersen's chest. Some think they saw Tandefelt but are uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several of those questioned say it was a person in sailor's clothes but describe clothes different from the ones Tandefelt was wearing that day. None of these witnesses wanted to testify in court that Tandefelt had indeed been the
2 CONC  person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tandefelt himself and his landlady Elisabeth Almberg are both certain that he was wearing a green sweater and blue trousers both morning and afternoon. Police inspector Erik Henrik Kjellberg, who lived in the same house as Almberg
2 CONC , told the inquiry that Tandefelt had been wearing a sweater and blue trousers when he went out on the morning of the day in question. Kjellberg was prepared to testify to this under oath.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the murder of von Fersen, as many as 900 people were thoroughly questioned, but the investigation led to the identification of only around 30 people. In the end, just four people were brought before the Supreme Court on 
2 CONC 30 June: junior officer Georg Bartholin, the German-born wholesale merchant Johan Gottlieb Lexow, who was dressed in dark blue sailor's clothes at the time of the murder, the French-born actor Joseph August Lambert and the Finnish
2 CONC -born sailor Otto Johan Tandefelt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tandefelt was born on 15 April 1782 in Sysmä, at Hovila Manor belonging to the family. He attended Haapaniemi Cadet School for three years from 1798 to 1801. He then waited for his possible promotion to the rank of lieutenant by s
2 CONC erving for five years as a sergeant in the Jägerhorn Regiment in Turku until 1807. After that, he took a commission as a seaman on a ship to France and the Mediterranean. A few days before the Russian invasion of Finland on 21 Feb
2 CONC ruary 1808, he re-enlisted as a non-commissioned officer, a sergeant in the Uusimaa Infantry Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the regiment he was on a six-week retreat from Hämeenlinna to Ostrobothnia after the early battles of the war. After Colonel Carl Johan Adlercreutz took command, the counter-offensive began, in which Tandefelt, in addition to
2 CONC  smaller skirmishes, took part in the famous and victorious battles of Siikajoki (16 and 18 April) and Lapua (14 July).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1808, the volunteer Sergeant Tandefelt, in preparation for the Russian invasion, became a plainclothes scout, using the pseudonym of Otto Johan Pettersson, and a handler of the reconnaissance network he had set up
2 CONC  in Greater Savo. To avoid being identified by the Russians searching for him, Otto Johan fled Finland in 1810 and arrived in Stockholm on 13 July. There, still using his alias, he quickly took a 10-month commission as a seaman on
2 CONC  board Captain Frese's ship, which was later bound for England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He spent the next week meeting his fellow soldiers and acquaintances and possibly reporting on Finnish affairs to the relevant authorities. Then came the events of 20 July with their consequences. There remained the question of gu
2 CONC ilt. The murder was followed by a trial, with an investigation in a special lower court (judgment of 20 March 1811); the case went on to the Swedish Court of Appeal (decision of 20 August the same year) and, some parts of it, to t
2 CONC he Supreme Court (decision of 21 November the same year).
2 CONT
2 CONT Bartholin was released. Lambert was sentenced to 16 days in prison with just bread and water for disobedience and drunkenness. Lexow was sentenced to life imprisonment in the fortress of Varberg (and after two years, following a p
2 CONC lea for clemency to Crown Prince Carl Johan, he was deported from the country).
2 CONT
2 CONT Tandefelt, on one hand, was said to have shouted that von Fersen should be killed, and on the other, "don't do it, but rather arrest him", and was considered to have participated in the riot, but could not be convicted of complici
2 CONC ty in the murder with his plea of "not guilty". Tandefelt was imprisoned in the fortress of Nya Älvsborg in Gothenburg in order that he would confess.
2 CONT
2 CONT After that, it was the harsh conditions of the fortress prison against Otto Johan's willpower. Tandefelt still did not confess to anything or reveal any other possible members of the conspiracy. After more than a year of pre-trial
2 CONC  detention in Stockholm and two earlier arrests by the Russians, he did not crack under this heavy interrogation either.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 April 1814, Otto Johan Tandefelt's unspecified prison sentence was commuted to deportation, following a plea for clemency to King Charles VIII, apparently initiated by the military authorities. In the process, he lost his ar
2 CONC istocratic title and his official surname, Tandefelt. He also received a further 28 days of imprisonment with just bread and water, possibly for disobedience, as he had failed to denounce the conspirators he was likely to have kno
2 CONC wn and had perhaps argued against the claims and, most importantly, refused to confess to an unproven crime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of May, he was escorted onto a cargo ship carrying timber which was bound for Spain. The lost Grand Duchy of Finland was not likely to have been considered a sufficiently distant foreign country. In June 1814, Otto
2 CONC  Johan Pettersson was finally a free man under the southern sun in the great port city of Cádiz on the west coast of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT After learning the ways and language of the country, initially as a stevedore, Otto Johan sought a job more suited to his character and enlisted in the army. In his early years, he progressed through the ranks quite rapidly, risin
2 CONC g to the rank of officer. After taking part in the 1820 rebellion with the troops led by Rafael del Riego y Nunez (1785-1823) on the side of those who supported the liberal constitution of 1812, which had been overturned by King F
2 CONC erdinand VII in 1814, he soon rose to the rank of captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The career of the famously competent officer continued even after the successful invasion of Spain by France in 1823 and the defeat of the Spanish under Rafael del Riego, which restored the King's absolute power. The lessons learn
2 CONC t in Finland, especially in tactics at Haapaniemi Cadet School, ahead of their time, and his experience as a professional soldier in wars were certainly useful in his new military career. Fighting in Spain at that time was to a la
2 CONC rge degree guerilla warfare.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1817, Otto Johan Pettersson, then an officer in the Spanish army, was visiting his old home country, Finland. The reason was the question of the inheritance of the Hovila and Voipala estates, which he and his moth
2 CONC er partly owned in Sysmä. Otto Johan's stepfather Vilhelm Boisman was seriously ill at the Raskula or Strandgård manor, where the family lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boisman intended to leave the estates or the money from them to his own children, Otto Johan's half-brothers and half-sisters. Negotiations and mediation were necessary, which required Otto Johan to make several visits to Sysmä. D
2 CONC uring his journey, he stayed at his former base in Mikkeli, at the prosperous Kyyhkylänniemi farm owned by Lieutenant Colonel Otto Carl von Fieandt (1758-1825).
2 CONT
2 CONT At the farm was Maria Kristina Dahlberg (1795-1842), the closest help and housekeeper of the family, who had moved from the household of Anna Sofia Borenia, a widowed dowager, from Orivesi earlier that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria and Otto Johan quickly developed a close relationship, which ended when Otto Johan returned to Spain in September 1817. The following year, on 11 May 1818, Maria Kristina gave birth to a son, who was named Carl Fredrik Dahlm
2 CONC an. The surname was changed to Pettersson after his mother's marriage in 1825 to the non-commissioned officer Pehr Gottfrid Pettersson and the family's move to Hartola on 20 December 1825.
2 CONT
2 CONT From there Carl (Kalle) Fredrik at the age of 19, recommended by Otto Johan’s mother Maria Christina Tandefelt before her death (20 December 1837), was hired on 30 October 1837 as a farmhand at Voipala Manor in Sysmä. Pehr Petters
2 CONC son had died of typhoid on 3 September 1836 and Maria Kristina was left with Kalle and at least two underage children. When Kalle married, at the age of 27, he was the assistant of the estate manager and foreman of the other farmh
2 CONC ands at Virta Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kalle’s wife was Maria Erkintytär (Ericsdaughter), born in Sysmä on 22 February 1820. Kalle and Maria are the ancestors of the branches of the Pettersson family of Sysmä. On the small hereditary farm Kaunisranta in Rusutjärvi, Tuu
2 CONC sula, lived the master painter Johan Henrik Pettersson (1854-1917) and Gustava, née Tulokas (1851-1931) with eight children (from 1906 the Finnish surname was Mannerheimo). It was only tacitly known among the close adult relatives
2 CONC  that the father's real grandfather was Otto Johan Pettersson, an officer who was once surrounded by many mythical stories.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1825, Captain Otto Johan Pettersson attempted to return to Finland by boat via Copenhagen and Stockholm. It is entirely possible that the drastic political changes in Spain at the time, his age of 43 and a dozen y
2 CONC ears of sometimes arduous service in the Spanish army made the old home country seem attractive again.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Swedish capital, Otto Johan once again daringly contacted his old allies in the conspiracy. Apparently, he made careless moves and was arrested. He could of course, be suspected of being involved in new political plots and 
2 CONC conspiracies against the state. The arrest and interrogation in Stockholm on this occasion lasted for months and he was even threatened with death penalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto Johan was ultimately saved by his Spanish citizenship, the documents he had with him proving he was a Spanish captain and the impending diplomatic conflict between the two countries. However, there was nothing to prevent Capt
2 CONC ain Otto Johan Pettersson from being returned to Gádiz in Spain. Very little is known about his later life in Spain or elsewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT The information that he was about to emigrate to America and that at one point he had feared a possible yellow fever epidemic came probably from three letters received by relatives in the latter part of the decade. Captain Otto Jo
2 CONC han Pettersson's final years, at least for the time being, are lost in the waves of life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ilari Langinkoski/RB
